{
  "description": "Certificates in Certificate Transparency logs and the alarms of a gateway, RELAY-v0.md section 6.7. Each case: the public names of the installation, the time of the pairing and the time of the check, the gateway's server keys (the current one, and earlier ones with the time each was replaced), the certificates the gateway has acknowledged (by digest only), and the certificates found in the logs; each with its digest, its names, its validity, the SHA-256 digest of its public key and the time of its first log entry. Result for each certificate, in the order given: alarm (the gateway raises an alarm), past-exposure (the certificate has expired and is reported as a past exposure) or none. A certificate counts for a public name if one of its names matches it without regard to case, or is a wildcard of one label, in the leftmost position, that covers it. A certificate is valid since the pairing if its validity, bounds included, overlaps the time from the pairing to the check. A certificate of the current server key is the gateway's own; one of an earlier key only if it was logged before that key was replaced (strictly). The digest of a certificate stands for the SHA-256 digest of the DER of its TBSCertificate with the poison and the SCT list removed, so that a precertificate and its certificate have one digest; in the cases it is the SHA-256 digest of the strings tbs-x, tbs-y, tbs-p, tbs-w and tbs-g, in canonical base64url. A certificate that is not valid yet (its notBefore is after the time of the check) raises an alarm when its notAfter is not before the time of the check. Public keys are the SHA-256 digests of the strings pin-server-current, pin-server-old, pin-foreign and pin-foreign-2, in canonical base64url.",
